Lysosomal storage diseases
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Progressive neurodegeneration/skill loss, Coarse facial features, Hepatosplenomegaly, Lysosomal enzyme deficiency

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-029
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory failure, severe hypoxia, and suspected aspiration pneumonia.
Location: Private residence (Palliative setup)
Present at Visit: Patient, parents, and community nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 6-year-old child with a known advanced lysosomal storage disease presenting with sudden, severe difficulty breathing, deep blue discoloration of the lips, and thick, unmanageable airway secretions over the past 2 hours. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the critical, life-threatening nature of the airway compromise in a patient with severe hypotonia and skeletal deformities, requiring immediate in-home medical stabilization and aggressive secretion clearance to prevent respiratory arrest.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He is turning blue, his chest is heaving, and we can't clear the thick glue from his throat.
History of Present Illness: The community nurse reports a 2-hour history of acute respiratory decompensation in a child with a known lysosomal enzyme deficiency. The patient has a history of progressive neurodegeneration leading to total loss of previously acquired motor skills. The current episode began with a sudden increase in thick, copious upper airway secretions that the patient cannot clear due to severe hypotonia progressing to spasticity. Over the last hour, the patient developed severe intercostal retractions and central cyanosis. The child has a history of recurrent severe respiratory infections and skeletal deformities (severe kyphoscoliosis) that severely restrict lung expansion. He also suffers from intractable epilepsy, though no seizures have occurred today.
Functional Status: Bedbound, entirely dependent; receiving specialized palliative-oriented care.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of terminal respiratory arrest; profound parental distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/50 mmHg | HR: 165 bpm | Temp: 38.6 C | RR: 48 bpm | SpO2: 82% (Room Air) | Wt: 18.0 Kg
General: Child is lethargic, centrally cyanotic, and in severe respiratory distress with marked tracheal tug and nasal flaring. Coarse facial features are prominent.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals severe tachypnea and shallow tidal volumes restricted by profound kyphoscoliosis. Auscultation reveals loud, transmitted upper airway rhonchi (secretions) and focal coarse crackles at the right lung base, highly suspicious for acute aspiration or bacterial pneumonia. Abdominal exam reveals massive hepatosplenomegaly causing significant abdominal distension, further impeding diaphragmatic excursion. Neurological exam confirms profound hypotonia and unresponsiveness (baseline severe cognitive impairment with corneal clouding).
Environmental Observations: Home suction machine is running continuously but struggling with the viscosity of the secretions.
Medication Reconciliation: Regular anti-epileptics given; PRN sublingual hyoscine given 1 hour ago without sufficient drying effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lysosomal storage diseases (complicated by acute severe aspiration pneumonia and respiratory failure) - Status: Acute, critical complication
Differentials:

Status Epilepticus (Non-convulsive) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of the immediate hypoxia, because the mechanical airway obstruction (secretions) and focal lung crackles provide a direct, structural cause for the respiratory failure.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely immediately, although lysosomal accumulation in the myocardium occurs; the high fever, purulent secretions, and focal right-sided crackles point strongly to an infectious/aspiration etiology.

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of bilateral (though restricted) breath sounds and the overwhelming presence of wet secretions, rather than sudden unilateral absent breath sounds.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Life-threatening emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, critical respiratory failure (SpO2 82%) and severe airway obstruction secondary to an inability to manage secretions. The objective findings of high fever, focal lung crackles, central cyanosis, and severe tachypnea in a patient with massive hepatosplenomegaly and kyphoscoliosis confirmed a decompensated aspiration pneumonia. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because the child was minutes away from hypoxic cardiac arrest. Immediate, aggressive medical intervention was required to establish a patent airway, provide high-flow oxygen, and facilitate emergency transport for intravenous antibiotics and potential non-invasive ventilation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately commenced high-flow supplemental oxygen via non-rebreather mask (improving SpO2 to 91%). Performed deep, aggressive oropharyngeal suctioning using a larger bore Yankauer catheter to remove thick mucus plugs. Administered stat intramuscular Ceftriaxone to initiate broad-spectrum antibiotic coverage immediately. Activated 000 for emergency ambulance transport to the Paediatric Intensive Care Unit.
Positive Behavioral Change: N/A - Acute life-threatening emergency requiring immediate transfer.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedic crew and receiving pediatric critical care registrar.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently being transported to the Emergency Department via ambulance under my direct supervision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying disease is progressive and limits physiological reserve; recovery from severe acute infections is often prolonged and incomplete.
Clinical Course: The child will require intensive care admission for IV antibiotics, aggressive chest physiotherapy, and specialized respiratory support to overcome the pneumonia and clear the secretions.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure all home respiratory and suction equipment is heavily cleaned and serviced while the child is in the hospital to prevent re-infection upon discharge.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: His severe muscle weakness and curved spine make it impossible for him to cough up the thick mucus. This caused the mucus to slide into his right lung, creating a severe infection that is blocking oxygen from reaching his blood. The deep suctioning cleared the main airway, and the oxygen mask is keeping him alive until the hospital can provide advanced breathing support.
Actionable Steps: Pack his complex medical records and advance care directives for the hospital. Follow the paramedics exactly.

